# Monitoring Architecture

## Overview

CloudWatch alarms watch the compute and data tiers; SNS fans out a
single email notification channel. No dashboards or log aggregation
are in scope for this project — see Future Improvements below.

## Alarms

| Alarm | Metric | Threshold | Trigger condition |
|---|---|---|---|
| `asg-high-cpu` | `AWS/EC2` `CPUUtilization`, averaged across the ASG | > 80% | 2 consecutive 5-min periods |
| `rds-high-cpu` | `AWS/RDS` `CPUUtilization` | > 80% | 2 consecutive 5-min periods |
| `rds-low-storage` | `AWS/RDS` `FreeStorageSpace` | < 2 GiB | 1 evaluation period |

All three publish to the same SNS topic (`alerts`) on alarm; the CPU
alarms also notify on return to OK, so a resolved incident is visible
without having to check the console.

## SNS Subscription

- Single email subscription (`var.notification_email`).
- AWS sends a confirmation link to that address after `terraform
  apply` — the subscription stays `PendingConfirmation` and delivers
  nothing until it's clicked. This is an AWS SNS behavior, not
  something Terraform can complete unattended.

## Why Not More

This project intentionally keeps monitoring to "would page someone
for an actual problem" — CPU exhaustion and DB storage exhaustion are
the two failure modes most likely to actually occur in a small,
Multi-AZ, auto-scaled setup like this one. A production deployment
would add:

- ALB target 5xx rate and unhealthy host count alarms
- RDS `FreeableMemory` and connection-count alarms
- Centralized logging (CloudWatch Logs / a log aggregator) — none of
  the app or infra logs are shipped anywhere in this repo today
- A CloudWatch dashboard instead of relying on individual alarms

## Terraform Mapping

`terraform/monitoring.tf` — `aws_sns_topic`,
`aws_sns_topic_subscription`, and the three
`aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm` resources listed above.